== 12. Of the Wife

The wife is the other married person, who, being subject to her husband, yields obedience to him.

The word of God mentions the subjection of the wife in various places. Rom 7:2: “A woman in subjection to a husband is bound by law to her husband as long as he lives.” Eph 5:24: “As the church submits to Christ, so also wives should submit to their husbands in everything.” Col 3:18: “Wives, submit to your#super[+] husbands, as is fitting in the Lord.” 1 Tim 2:12: “I do not permit a woman … to exercise authority over a man.” And it was a law God established immediately after the fall, Gen 3:16: “To the woman He said: ‘I will sharply increase … Your desire will be subject to your husband, and he will rule over you.’” Indeed, according to the civil law, the daughter, even when she is married, is#footnote[In potestate patris.] #emph[in the power of her father,] and not of her husband. But this is directly against the law of Moses, and goes against the law of nature, Lev 22:12, 13; Num 30:13.

Now the duties of the wife are chiefly two.

The first is to submit herself to her husband, and to acknowledge and reverence him as her head in all things. Gen 20:16: in the same way Abimelech said to Sarah, “See, your brother” (that is, your husband, whom you call your brother) “is a veil for your eyes before all who are with you.” As if he should say, #emph[Your husband is your head, and has power over you, and you ought to reverence him.] For in ancient times the wife was covered with a veil in the presence of her husband, as a token of subjection to him. So Rebekah, at the sight of Isaac, took her veil “and covered herself” (Gen 24:65). 1 Cor 11:3: “The head of the woman is man.” Eph 5:22: “Wives, submit to your#super[+] husbands as to the Lord. For the husband is the head of the wife as Christ is the head of the church.” The reason for this is good. For the wife enjoys the privileges of her husband, and is graced by his honor and standing among men. His nobility makes her noble, though otherwise she is of low and humble rank; as, on the other hand, his baseness and low degree cause her to be low and humble in rank, though she is noble and honorable by birth.

The second duty is to be obedient to her husband in all things; that is, to depend wholly on him, both in judgment and in will. For just as the church yields obedience to Christ her head, and yields herself to be commanded, governed and directed by him, so ought the woman to the man. So Sarah is said to have “obeyed Abraham,” and to have given him the terms of obedience: she “called him lord,” or sir (1 Pet 3:6). From this it follows that the woman is not to take the liberty of wandering and straying abroad from her own house without the man’s knowledge and consent. 2 Kgs 4:22: “And the woman called her husband and said, ‘Please send me one of the servants and one of the donkeys, that I may go quickly to the man of God and return.’” Again, it follows that she is to follow her husband when he moves or departs from place to place, unless he forsakes either her or Christ. To this purpose Paul says that he had the “right to take along a believing wife, as do the other apostles” (1 Cor 9:5). So Sarah went with Abraham into Egypt, Gen 12:11; and out of Egypt, Gen 13:1; and to Gerar, to sojourn there, Gen 20:1, 2, 3. So too the wives of Jacob departed with him from their father Laban, Gen 31:17.

#emph[Contrary to these duties are the sins of wives: to be proud; to be unwilling to bear the authority of their husbands; to scold and quarrel bitterly; to forsake their houses; and the like.] Prov 27:15: “A constant dripping on a rainy day and a contentious woman are alike.” v. 16: “Restraining her is like holding back the wind or grasping oil with one’s right hand.” Exod 4:26: “When she said, ‘bridegroom of blood,’ she was referring to the circumcision.” #emph[It was the fault of the Levite’s concubine,] Judg 19:2: “She was unfaithful to him and left him to return to her father’s house in Bethlehem in Judah,” and she was there four months. #emph[It was the sin of] Queen Vashti, who “refused to come at the king’s command brought by his eunuchs. And the king became furious, and his anger burned within him” (Esth 1:12). #emph[Lastly, to be a cause of grief to their kindred. So the proud wives of Esau, the daughters of the Hittites, were a grief of mind to Isaac and Rebekah,] Gen 26:35; 27:46.
